  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "The growth rate of the deer population $P(t)$ in a certain forest is jointly proportional to the deer population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the forest's carrying capacity and the deer population. The constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,03$ and the carrying capacity is $9000.$ How many deers will there be in the forest after $8$ years given that there were $1800$ deers initially? Round your answer to the nearest integer.",
  "worked_solution": "Since the deer population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the deer population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the deer population, the deer population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,03,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=9000.$ Therefore, the deer population growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,03P(9000-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{9000}{1+C{e}^{-(9000\\cdot 0.000\\,03)t}}=\\frac{9000}{1+C{e}^{-0.27t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=1800$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =1800 \\\\ \\frac{9000}{1+C{e}^{-0.27(0)}} & =1800 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{9000}{1800} \\\\ C & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{9000}{1+4{e}^{-0.27\\cdot t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=8$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(8) & =\\frac{9000}{1+4{e}^{-0.27\\cdot 8}} \\\\  & \u22486159.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
